# Is the relation between socioeconomic status and chances of degree completion affected by racial lines?

## Written hypothesis (data-grounded answer)

**Among NLS respondents, socioeconomic status is positively associated with the chance of completing a BA
degree, but this socioeconomic gradient does not appear to be modified by the race of the individual once
background ability, sex and family size are held constant.** Each additional standard deviation of SES raises
the odds of degree completion by about 1.23 (OR 1.23, 95% CI 1.05-1.45, p = 0.013), and the estimated
SES slope is statistically indistinguishable across the three racial groups: adding an SES-by-race interaction
to the total-effect model improves the fit by nothing beyond chance (LRT chi2(2) = 1.33, p = 0.513;
Wald chi2(2) = 1.37, p = 0.503; AIC worsens by 2.67; parametric-bootstrap p = 0.514; Firth-penalised
Wald p = 0.478). Race instead enters **chiefly as a difference in level rather than a difference in slope**:
Black and Hispanic respondents complete degrees at markedly lower rates than White respondents overall
(1.32% and 1.44% vs 3.54%; unadjusted ORs 0.42 and 0.49), and race retains an independent association with
completion after adjustment (joint LRT chi2(2) = 6.10, p = 0.047), an association whose sign *reverses* once
ability, sex and family size are held constant (Black OR 1.86, 95% CI 1.20-2.89, p = 0.006; Hispanic OR 1.34,
95% CI 0.81-2.23, p = 0.26) — i.e. given the same SES, ability, sex and family size, Black respondents in this
sample show higher, not lower, modelled chances of a degree.

An SES-by-race interaction *is* visible in the unadjusted data (crude LRT chi2(2) = 9.57, p = 0.008; crude
SES ORs per SD: White 1.17, Hispanic 1.65, Black 2.07), but it does not survive adjustment and should not be
read as effect modification: within the minority groups SES tracks ability and family size differently than
within the White group, so part of the crude slope gap is compositional. Because the analysis plan pre-specified
the adjusted total-effect test as decisive, this crude result is reported descriptively and is not promoted to a
claim of effect modification.

**Where the evidence is thin.** The Hispanic-by-SES term is imprecisely estimated and rests on 19 degrees among
1,323 respondents (OR per SD 1.19, 95% CI 0.75-1.88); it is not distinguishable from the White slope, and it is
not shown to be equal to it. The study is underpowered for exactly this contrast: a difference in slope would
need to be at least 0.60 log-odds per SD (an odds-ratio ratio of about 1.8) to be detected with 80% power,
against 0.45 log-odds (ratio about 1.6) for Black vs White. Removing the seven Hispanic respondents in the top
SES quartile who hold a degree moves the Hispanic slope from +0.17 to -0.42, which shows how little those
few records carry. The Black slope is the one group-specific slope that is significantly non-zero in its own
right (OR per SD 1.50, 95% CI 1.04-2.17), yet its *difference* from the White slope (0.23 log-odds,
p = 0.25) is not significant.

