## Robustness checks (all pre-specified or labelled as sensitivities)

* Categorical SES (quartiles) x race, 6 df: chi2 = 1.47, p = 0.961; SES^2 x race: chi2(2) = 0.63, p = 0.730;
  curvature within the White reference group p = 0.418 (linear entry adequate).
* Re-levelling to Black as reference reproduces the identical LRT (1.333, p = 0.513) - the joint test does not
  depend on the choice of reference category.
* Firth penalised likelihood (236 events / 8,773 records): interaction Wald p = 0.478; slopes White 0.173
  (p = 0.062), Black 0.412 (p = 0.025), Hispanic 0.175 (p = 0.441). The penalised *likelihood-ratio* statistic
  (7.43) is reported but deliberately not compared with chi2, since the penalty 0.5*log|I| does not cancel
  between models of different dimension.
* Parametric (model-based) bootstrap of the interaction LRT, B = 800: p = 0.514; simulated null 95th percentile
  6.56 vs theoretical 5.99, so the chi2(2) reference is adequate and no small-sample correction reverses the verdict.
* Fully race-specific model (all slopes x race): extra slope differences chi2(6) = 8.64, p = 0.195.
* Stratified fits: White OR 1.26 (p = 0.019), Black OR 1.51 (p = 0.042), Hispanic OR 0.98 (p = 0.92).
* Pooled "minority vs White" contrast (labelled sensitivity only): interaction p = 0.366.
* SES x sex (exploratory, not part of the primary test): p = 0.949.

## Assumptions and limitations

1. **PERCENTILE IN CLASS read as higher = better class standing** (working assumption, accepted by the researcher).
   Sign behaves sensibly once fitted: beta = +0.605 per SD (OR 1.83, p = 2e-07) in the partially direct model.
   The variable was **not** re-signed after seeing any result.
2. **Two estimands kept separate.** Total effect = adjusted for ability, sex, family size (headline).
   Partially direct = additionally adjusted for class percentile, a plausible mediator; judged on the total effect,
   as agreed. Both interaction tests are non-significant, so the headline does not depend on the mediator adjustment.
3. Ability is treated as a confounder and family size as an upstream family-background input; both are debatable
   adjustments, and the sign reversal of the race terms under adjustment (see the hypothesis) is exactly the kind
   of result that such an adjustment can produce. Race terms are not interpreted causally.
4. The Hispanic stratum (1,323 records, 19 events) is retained as its own level, never collapsed and never dropped;
   its interaction term is described as imprecise, and the 2-df joint test is treated as primary.
5. PERCENTILE/curriculum limitation: **no curriculum variable exists in the processed file**, although the study
   description promises one, so academic-programme rigour cannot be controlled; class percentile proxies it imperfectly.
6. **Design and generalisability.** The file is a complete-case subset (8,773 of the NLS cohort; no missing values
   in any analysed variable), and it supplies no survey weights, strata or primary sampling units. Inference is
   therefore made under an assumed simple-random-sampling design; standard errors are likely understated relative
   to the true complex-sample design, and generalisation beyond "respondents with valid class rank and an observed
   degree status" is not supported. No survey-style precision is claimed.
7. Association, not causation: nothing here licenses a causal reading of either the SES gradient or race.
